  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Dual Spaces and the Hahn-Banach Theorem\n\nFor a normed space $X$, the **dual** $X^* = \\mathcal{B}(X, \\mathbb{F})$ is the Banach space of bounded linear functionals on $X$. It's *always* a Banach space, even when $X$ isn't complete.\n\nThe dual measures $X$ from the outside: every continuous linear functional probes a particular projection of $X$. The richer $X^*$, the more the dual sees.\n\n**Hahn-Banach** is the theorem that guarantees $X^*$ is rich enough to separate points: every non-zero $x \\in X$ has *some* $\\varphi \\in X^*$ with $\\varphi(x) \\ne 0$. Without it, dual spaces could be trivial. With it, the whole apparatus of weak topologies, reflexivity, and abstract duality theory falls into place.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 19,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Hahn-Banach extension\n\n**Statement**. Let $X$ be a real or complex normed space, $M \\subseteq X$ a subspace, and $\\varphi_0 : M \\to \\mathbb{F}$ a bounded linear functional with norm $\\|\\varphi_0\\|_M = c$. Then $\\varphi_0$ extends to a bounded linear functional $\\varphi : X \\to \\mathbb{F}$ with $\\|\\varphi\\|_X = c$.\n\n**Consequences**:\n\n- **Separation of points**: every $x \\ne 0$ has a $\\varphi$ with $\\varphi(x) = \\|x\\| \\ne 0$.\n- **Distance to subspace**: $d(x, M) = \\sup_{\\varphi \\perp M, \\|\\varphi\\| = 1} |\\varphi(x)|$.\n- **Geometric form**: a closed convex set and a point outside it can be separated by a hyperplane.\n\nThe proof uses Zorn's lemma (axiom of choice) — there's no constructive proof for general $X$, but for separable $X$ (countable dense subset) the construction is more concrete.\n\n## Duals of $\\ell^p$ and $L^p$ — the conjugate exponent\n\nFor $1 \\le p < \\infty$ with conjugate $q = p/(p-1)$ (so $1/p + 1/q = 1$), the dual of $L^p$ is $L^q$ via\n\n$$\\varphi_g(f) = \\int f g\\,d\\mu, \\qquad \\|\\varphi_g\\| = \\|g\\|_q.$$\n\nSpecial case $p = q = 2$: $L^2$ is its own dual (Riesz, Stage 5/02).\n\nThe dual of $L^\\infty$ is bigger than $L^1$ (it includes finitely additive measures). $L^1$ and $L^\\infty$ are not \"reflexive\" — applying the dual twice doesn't return to the original space.\n\n## Reflexive spaces\n\nA Banach space $X$ is **reflexive** if the canonical map $J : X \\to X^{**}$, $J(x)(\\varphi) = \\varphi(x)$, is surjective (it's always isometric).\n\n- $L^p$ for $1 < p < \\infty$ is reflexive.\n- $\\ell^p$ for $1 < p < \\infty$ is reflexive.\n- Hilbert spaces are reflexive.\n- $L^1, L^\\infty, C([a, b]), \\ell^1, \\ell^\\infty$ are **not** reflexive.\n\nReflexivity makes optimisation easier: bounded sequences in a reflexive space have weakly-convergent subsequences (Banach-Alaoglu + Eberlein-Šmulian). Many existence theorems for PDE rely on this.\n\n## Concrete: Riesz on $\\ell^2$\n\n$\\ell^2$ is its own dual. Every bounded linear functional is $\\varphi(x) = \\sum x_n \\bar y_n$ for some $y \\in \\ell^2$. Compute $\\|\\varphi\\|$ for $y = (1, 1/2, 1/3, \\ldots)$:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* y = (1/n) for n = 1, 2, 3, ... — already shown to be in ℓ², not ℓ¹ */\nsimpsum : true$   /* without this Maxima leaves the infinite sum unevaluated */\ny_norm_sq : sum(1/n^2, n, 1, inf)$\nprint(\"‖y‖_2² = Σ 1/n² =\", y_norm_sq, \"=\", float(y_norm_sq))$\nprint(\"‖y‖_2 = π/√6 =\", float(%pi/sqrt(6)))$\nprint()$\nprint(\"Functional φ(x) = Σ x_n / n on ℓ² has ‖φ‖ = ‖y‖_2 = π/√6 (Riesz).\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 20,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "‖y‖_2² = Σ 1/n² = %pi^2/6 = 1.6449340668482262\n‖y‖_2 = π/√6 = 1.2825498301618643\nFunctional φ(x) = Σ x_n / n on ℓ² has ‖φ‖ = ‖y‖_2 = π/√6 (Riesz)."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Geometric Hahn-Banach: separating hyperplanes\n\nIf $A, B$ are disjoint convex subsets of a real normed space $X$ with $A$ open, there exists $\\varphi \\in X^*$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that\n\n$$\\varphi(a) < \\alpha \\le \\varphi(b), \\qquad \\forall a \\in A, b \\in B.$$\n\nIn words: a hyperplane (level set $\\{\\varphi = \\alpha\\}$) separates the two sets.\n\nThis is the abstract version of the **separation theorems** that power optimisation duality, support vector machines, and the basic theorems of linear programming.\n\n## Visualising in $\\mathbb{R}^2$\n\nTwo convex sets and the separating line:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Convex set A: disc around (-1, 0); convex set B: half-plane x >= 1 (illustrate as a region) */\ntheta_g : np_to_list(np_linspace(0, 2*float(%pi), 200))$\nA_x : map(lambda([t], -1.0 + 0.7*cos(t)), theta_g)$\nA_y : map(lambda([t], 0.5*sin(t)), theta_g)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=2, name=\"convex set A (disc)\",\n  lines(A_x, A_y),\n  marker_size=4, color=\"red\", name=\"convex set B (right of x=1)\",\n  points([1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0],\n         [0.0, 0.5, 0.0, -0.5, -0.5, 0.5, -0.3, 0.3]),\n  color=\"green\", dash=\"dash\", line_width=2, name=\"separating hyperplane\",\n  lines([0.5, 0.5], [-1.5, 1.5]),\n  title=\"Hahn-Banach separation: a line splits two disjoint convex sets\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"y\",\n  grid=true, aspect_ratio=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 21,
      "outputs": [
        {
