    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The line $x = 0.5$ has $A$ to its left and $B$ to its right. The functional $\\varphi(x, y) = x$ achieves the separation: $\\varphi(a) < 0.5 \\le \\varphi(b)$ for all $a \\in A, b \\in B$.\n\n## Weak and weak-* topologies\n\nThe **weak topology** on $X$ is the coarsest topology making every $\\varphi \\in X^*$ continuous. The **weak-* topology** on $X^*$ is the coarsest making evaluations $\\varphi \\mapsto \\varphi(x)$ continuous for each $x \\in X$.\n\n**Banach-Alaoglu**: the closed unit ball of $X^*$ is **weak-* compact**. This is the basic compactness theorem of functional analysis — bounded sequences in $X^*$ have weak-* convergent subnets.\n\nThese notions look abstract but are everywhere in PDE existence theory, calculus of variations, and probability (the weak-* topology on probability measures).\n\n## Where this leaves us\n\nThree big results, all powered by Hahn-Banach:\n\n1. **Separation of points**: $X^*$ is rich enough to detect all of $X$.\n2. **Reflexivity** distinguishes \"nice\" Banach spaces ($L^p, 1 < p < \\infty$, Hilbert) from \"harder\" ones ($L^1, L^\\infty, C[a,b]$).\n3. **Geometric separation**: every duality theorem in optimisation traces back to this.\n\nNext: the three \"big theorems\" of functional analysis (open mapping, closed graph, uniform boundedness), which are about the structure of bounded operators rather than functionals.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nConway, J.B. (2007) *A Course in Functional Analysis*. 2nd edn. New York, NY: Springer New York. doi:[10.1007/978-1-4757-4383-8](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-4383-8) — chs. III–IV (Hahn–Banach and its geometric consequences).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
